{name?, before?, after?, rename?, delete?, into?, now?, overrides?} the project's phases, in order, as Revit's Phasing does: a project starts with Existing and New Construction. `name` alone adds a phase at the end (or `before` or `after` another); with `rename`, renames it (its elements, views and templates follow); with `delete`, takes it out, its elements made and taken down in `into` instead (needed while anything holds it). An element is made in a phase (its `Phase Created` parameter, the first unless it says) and may be taken down in a later one (`Phase Demolished`), set with `modify {params}`; `now` (a phase, or null) is the phase what this session's commands make is made in (the first phase leaves no value). A view shows a phase and a phase filter (`graphics {phase, phase_filter}`): what's made after it isn't there, what's taken down before it neither, and the rest is new (made in it), existing, demolished (taken down in it) or temporary (both), each shown as its phase filter says (see `phase_filter`). `overrides` ({new|existing|demolished|temporary: override | null}, merged: an override as `graphics` has it, without hidden) is how a phase filter draws what it says overridden; the first two statuses start halftone and dashed red. Replies with the phases, `now` and the overrides

//! What the style and settings commands share: reading their parameters with the errors the
//! original gives, colours and pens, and the library entries that name the project's styles.

use crate::json::{Map, Value};
use crate::project::Project;

pub type R<T> = Result<T, String>;

/// Refuses a parameter the command doesn't take: "`x` isn't one of its parameters: a, b, c", the
/// first such in sorted order (the original's parameters are a sorted map).
pub fn check_keys(p: &Value, cmd: &str, allowed: &[&str]) -> R<()> {
    if let Value::Obj(m) = p {
        let mut keys: Vec<&String> = m.items.iter().map(|(k, _)| k).collect();
        keys.sort();
        for k in keys {
            if !allowed.contains(&k.as_str()) {
                return Err(format!("{}: `{}` isn't one of its parameters: {}", cmd, k, allowed.join(", ")));
            }
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}

/// A text parameter, trimmed: None when absent, null or blank.
pub fn opt_name(p: &Value, cmd: &str, key: &str) -> R<Option<String>> {
    match p.get(key) {
        None | Some(Value::Null) => Ok(None),
        Some(Value::Str(s)) if s.chars().count() <= 256 => {
            let t = s.trim();
            if t.is_empty() {
                Ok(None)
            } else {
                Ok(Some(t.to_string()))
            }
        }
        Some(v) => Err(format!("{}: `{}` must be text of at most 256 characters, got {}", cmd, key, v.to_compact())),
    }
}

/// `true` or `false` (null or absent: None).
pub fn opt_bool(p: &Value, cmd: &str, key: &str) -> R<Option<bool>> {
    crate::commands::params::opt_bool(p, cmd, key)
}

/// serde's "invalid type" message for a value that should have been of `expected` (f64, u8…).
pub fn invalid_type(v: &Value, expected: &str) -> String {
    format!("invalid type: {}, expected {}", v.kind_name(), expected)
}

/// A number as serde reads an f64 (any JSON number), else serde's error.
pub fn f64_of(v: &Value) -> Result<f64, String> {
    match v {
        Value::Num(n) => Ok(n.as_f64()),
        _ => Err(invalid_type(v, "f64")),
    }
}

/// A float as Rust's Display shows it (1.5, 17, 100000000000000000000).
pub fn disp(x: f64) -> String {
    format!("{}", x)
    if s == [0; 4] {
        s[0] = 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15;
    }
    s
}

/// xoshiro256** over the seed.
pub fn next_u64() -> u64 {
    STATE.with(|st| {
        let mut s = st.borrow_mut();
        let result = s[1].wrapping_mul(5).rotate_left(7).wrapping_mul(9);
        let t = s[1] << 17;
        s[2] ^= s[0];
        s[3] ^= s[1];
        s[1] ^= s[2];
        s[0] ^= s[3];
        s[2] ^= t;
        s[3] = s[3].rotate_left(45);
        result
    })
}

/// A random (version 4) GUID, lowercase and hyphenated.
pub fn guid() -> String {
    let a = next_u64();
    let b = next_u64();
    let mut bytes = [0u8; 16];
    bytes[..8].copy_from_slice(&a.to_be_bytes());
    bytes[8..].copy_from_slice(&b.to_be_bytes());
    bytes[6] = (bytes[6] & 0x0f) | 0x40;
    bytes[8] = (bytes[8] & 0x3f) | 0x80;
    let h: String = bytes.iter().map(|b| format!("{:02x}", b)).collect();
    format!("{}-{}-{}-{}-{}", &h[0..8], &h[8..12], &h[12..16], &h[16..20], &h[20..32])
}

/// Random alphanumeric characters, as tempfile names its files.
pub fn alnum(n: usize) -> String {
    const CHARS: &[u8] = b"ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789";
    (0..n).map(|_| CHARS[(next_u64() % CHARS.len() as u64) as usize] as char).collect()
}

